  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnother pleasant day this afternoon with slightly below normal\ntemperatures and some high level clouds streaming in aloft. \n\nSoutheastern Arizona's next weather system is evident on water\nvapor imagery digging south through the Great Basin into central\nCalifornia. This will continue to drop south into Arizona on\nFriday, with the wave deepened by an attendant jet streak over\nnorthern Sonora-Chihuahua. Guidance has come into better\nagreement with the strength of this low, which will produce\nbroad ascent over southeastern Arizona as well as a modest\nmoisture draw in to the region. This limited moisture field with\nprecipitable water values near 0.75 inches and surface dew \npoints in the 40s to lower 50s will combine with good cooling \naloft to produce a swath of 250 to near 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE \non Friday. In this band of instability, mainly from Tucson to \nNogales eastward, scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to develop in the afternoon through the evening hours.\nThese chances are greatest in Cochise through eastern \nGraham/Greenlee counties, with recent high-resolution guidance \nhighlighting a potential for isolated rainfall totals in Cochise\ncounty to exceed 0.5 inches with a very isolated shot at 1+ \ninches. Most areas should see less than a quarter inch of rain \nbut with sustained support aloft from the incoming shortwave, \nrepeated moderate rain showers or a brief heavy downpour may \nproduce a localized flash flooding threat. In collaboration with\nWPC, a marginal risk for excessive rainfall will be in effect \non Friday.\n\nThunderstorm chances should diminish through the evening, \nthough isolated showers will continue to be possible through \nearly Saturday morning in far eastern areas as the upper wave \nmoves through. Cold temperatures aloft and lingering moisture \nmay cause some weak instability and diurnally driven showers \nSaturday afternoon on the back side of the shortwave, but \npredictability at any one point is low and the forecast remains \nmostly dry for now. East winds will increase Saturday morning \nwith a tightening pressure gradient, especially in areas most \nsusceptible to east flow. In these areas sustained winds of \n15-20 mph winds with gusts to 30 mph are likely, with 10-15 mph \nand gusts to 25 mph elsewhere. East wind will continue through \nSunday, with weaker winds on Monday.\n\nModel solutions diverge next week but overall the forecast\nremains dry through much of the week. A signal for western\ntroughing shows up next Friday into the weekend, which should \nbe the next opportunity to start to bring at least low end\nprecipitation chances back into the forecast.",
